::CW_U03_L03_Q01::Source element: A character receives a warning before making a risky choice. Adaptation: A coder ignores a friend's warning before launching an app. What is preserved?{
~A. The ancient setting.#Incorrect. The setting is transformed.
=B. The warning before a risky choice.#Correct. The core source pattern remains.
~C. The exact original words.#Incorrect. No exact wording is preserved.
~D. The complete plot.#Incorrect. The plot situation changes.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q02::Source element: A guide gives a traveler a protective object. Adaptation: A coach gives a runner a worn shoelace before the final race. What is transformed?{
~A. The adaptation removes the guide.#Incorrect. The coach serves as a guide.
~B. The adaptation copies every event.#Incorrect. It changes setting and situation.
~C. The traveler becomes a sailor.#Incorrect. The adaptation uses a runner.
=D. The protective object becomes a realistic athletic keepsake.#Correct. The source object changes form while keeping its emotional role.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q03::A student only changes the names in an older story and keeps the same plot, setting, conflict, and ending. What is the issue?{
=A. The adaptation may be too close to copying.#Correct. Adaptation requires meaningful transformation.
~B. The adaptation changes too much.#Incorrect. It changes very little.
~C. The adaptation has no source connection.#Incorrect. It is too close to the source.
~D. The adaptation is strongest because names changed.#Incorrect. Names alone are not enough.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q04::Source element: A hero returns home changed. Which adaptation is strongest?{
~A. A hero returns home in the same words as the source.#Incorrect. That is copying.
~B. A character never leaves or changes.#Incorrect. The source element disappears.
=C. A student returns from a volunteer trip and notices needs in her own neighborhood.#Correct. The return-with-new-understanding pattern is preserved and transformed.
~D. A list defines the word hero.#Incorrect. A definition is not an adaptation.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q05::Why should a writer be careful when adapting sacred or culturally important material?{
=A. Because respectful adaptation avoids stereotypes and careless distortion.#Correct. Careful treatment protects meaning and audience trust.
~B. Because adaptation must always mock the source.#Incorrect. Mockery is not required and can be harmful.
~C. Because no source element can ever be transformed.#Incorrect. Transformation is part of adaptation.
~D. Because punctuation is the only concern.#Incorrect. Respect and representation matter.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q06::Source element: A character is tested by temptation. Adaptation: A class treasurer finds an envelope of fundraiser money before buying concert tickets. What source element remains?{
~A. A sea voyage.#Incorrect. No voyage appears.
~B. A magical helper.#Incorrect. No helper appears.
~C. A royal family.#Incorrect. No royal family appears.
=D. The temptation test.#Correct. The character faces a choice between desire and integrity.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q07::Which sentence is the best adaptation rationale?{
~A. I changed things because I felt like it.#Incorrect. This does not explain a craft purpose.
=B. I changed the setting to a train station so the journey pattern feels modern and urgent.#Correct. It names a change and explains its effect.
~C. I used the same plot so I did not need to decide anything.#Incorrect. That is not purposeful transformation.
~D. I removed all source connections.#Incorrect. Then it is not a clear adaptation.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q08::Source element: A trickster exposes a character's greed. Adaptation: A prank email reveals a business club leader taking all the credit. What changed?{
~A. The source is copied exactly.#Incorrect. The medium and setting change.
~B. Greed is removed completely.#Incorrect. Greed remains central.
=C. The exposure of greed remains, but the trickster action becomes digital.#Correct. The source pattern is transformed into a modern context.
~D. The adaptation has no conflict.#Incorrect. The credit-taking creates conflict.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q09::What is the best first step when planning an adaptation?{
~A. Copy the ending first.#Incorrect. Copying is not the goal.
~B. Add random details with no source connection.#Incorrect. Random details weaken adaptation.
~C. Avoid thinking about purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose is essential.
=D. Identify one source element to preserve.#Correct. A clear source element gives the adaptation focus.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q10::Source element: A forbidden door tests curiosity. Adaptation: A student is told not to open a locked digital folder. What is preserved?{
=A. The forbidden-choice test.#Correct. The door becomes a digital folder, but the test remains.
~B. The exact physical door.#Incorrect. The door changes form.
~C. The source's original time period.#Incorrect. The adaptation is modern.
~D. Nothing from the source.#Incorrect. The test remains recognizable.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q11::Which adaptation choice most clearly changes point of view?{
~A. Keeping every sentence exactly the same.#Incorrect. That does not change point of view.
~B. Only changing the font.#Incorrect. Font is not point of view.
=C. Telling a familiar journey pattern from the guide's perspective instead of the hero's.#Correct. The narrator focus changes.
~D. Removing all characters.#Incorrect. That erases the story pattern.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q12::Source element: A community survives a flood by working together. Adaptation: Neighbors rebuild after a burst water main. What is the main transformation?{
~A. Cooperation is removed.#Incorrect. Neighbors rebuild together.
=B. A large mythic disaster becomes a local realistic emergency.#Correct. The cooperation theme remains, while the event changes.
~C. The setting stays ancient.#Incorrect. A water main suggests a modern setting.
~D. The source is copied word for word.#Incorrect. The situation changes.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q13::Which choice is too vague for an adaptation plan?{
=A. I will adapt something old somehow.#Correct. This does not identify a source element or transformation.
~B. I will preserve a guide's gift and transform it into a coach's note.#Incorrect. This is specific.
~C. I will keep the pride-before-fall pattern and set it in a robotics contest.#Incorrect. This is specific.
~D. I will transform a journey into a first day at a new school.#Incorrect. This names a transformation.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q14::Adaptation: A shy student answers a call to organize a neighborhood cleanup after a storm. What source pattern does this likely adapt?{
~A. A cooking recipe.#Incorrect. No cooking appears.
~B. A random object list.#Incorrect. The focus is action and responsibility.
~C. A spelling test.#Incorrect. No spelling test appears.
=D. A call to adventure or responsibility.#Correct. The student is called into action.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q15::Which statement best explains transformation?{
~A. Transformation means copying exactly.#Incorrect. Copying is not transformation.
~B. Transformation means removing every source connection.#Incorrect. Then the source is not recognizable.
=C. Transformation changes a source element for a new purpose while keeping a recognizable connection.#Correct. This defines adaptation accurately.
~D. Transformation only changes punctuation.#Incorrect. It changes craft elements meaningfully.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q16::Source element: Pride causes a fall. Adaptation: A gamer refuses advice, livestreams too early, and loses the tournament. What theme connection remains?{
~A. Advice is always useless.#Incorrect. The advice would have helped.
=B. Pride can make a character ignore useful guidance.#Correct. The modern conflict keeps the pride pattern.
~C. Tournaments have no stakes.#Incorrect. The loss creates stakes.
~D. Pride always helps.#Incorrect. Pride causes failure here.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q17::What should a rationale include?{
~A. Only the character's new name.#Incorrect. A name is not enough.
~B. A refusal to explain choices.#Incorrect. Rationale requires explanation.
~C. Only a plot summary of the source.#Incorrect. The adaptation choices matter too.
=D. Source element, transformation, and reason for the change.#Correct. These parts explain the adaptation.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q18::Source element: A character is guided through darkness by a small light. Adaptation: A phone flashlight leads a group through a power outage. What is preserved?{
=A. Guidance through darkness by light.#Correct. The light changes form but keeps its role.
~B. The exact original object.#Incorrect. The light becomes a phone flashlight.
~C. A royal setting.#Incorrect. No royal setting appears.
~D. No source pattern.#Incorrect. The light-guidance pattern is clear.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q19::Which adaptation is respectful and purposeful?{
~A. It mocks a sacred source for humor only.#Incorrect. That is not respectful.
=B. It focuses on a source pattern, avoids stereotypes, and explains why the change matters.#Correct. This meets adaptation and respect expectations.
~C. It uses stereotypes because they are quick.#Incorrect. Stereotypes are harmful and weak.
~D. It hides the source connection completely.#Incorrect. The adaptation should have a clear source element.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q20::Source element: A messenger brings news that changes a kingdom. Adaptation: A text message changes a student council's decision. What is transformed?{
~A. News is removed.#Incorrect. The text message carries news.
~B. Decision-making disappears.#Incorrect. The council's decision changes.
=C. The messenger becomes a text message, and the kingdom becomes student council.#Correct. The communication pattern remains while context changes.
~D. Everything is copied exactly.#Incorrect. Multiple elements are transformed.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q21::Why is evidence-based craft reasoning important in adaptation?{
=A. It lets the writer explain how specific choices connect to the source and new effect.#Correct. Evidence grounds the rationale.
~B. It replaces all creative choices.#Incorrect. It explains choices, not replaces them.
~C. It means no source can be changed.#Incorrect. Adaptation requires change.
~D. It only checks grammar.#Incorrect. It focuses on craft reasoning.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q22::Which adaptation plan keeps too little source connection?{
~A. I will adapt a warning pattern into a science fair conflict.#Incorrect. The source pattern remains.
~B. I will use a guide's gift as a coach's note.#Incorrect. The source role remains.
=C. I will write about lunch with no connection to any source element.#Correct. No recognizable source element remains.
~D. I will transform a journey into moving to a new city.#Incorrect. The journey pattern remains.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q23::Source element: A character must choose between duty and desire. Adaptation: A musician must choose between a solo audition and helping at a family event. What is preserved?{
~A. The original location.#Incorrect. The setting is transformed.
~B. The exact ending.#Incorrect. No exact ending is given.
~C. A battle scene.#Incorrect. No battle appears.
=D. The duty-versus-desire conflict.#Correct. The specific situation changes, but the conflict remains.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q24::Which choice best distinguishes adaptation from summary?{
~A. Adaptation and summary are the same.#Incorrect. They have different purposes.
=B. Adaptation transforms source elements into a new creative work; summary retells the source briefly.#Correct. This explains the difference.
~C. Summary always changes point of view.#Incorrect. Summary condenses.
~D. Adaptation never uses source elements.#Incorrect. It depends on source elements.
}
::CW_U03_L03_Q25::Adaptation: The source has a guide who gives a map. The new story has an older cousin who draws a bus route on a napkin. What is the best explanation?{
=A. The adaptation preserves guidance through an object but transforms the object and setting.#Correct. The map becomes a bus-route napkin in a modern context.
~B. The adaptation removes guidance.#Incorrect. The cousin guides the character.
~C. The adaptation copies exactly.#Incorrect. The details change.
~D. The adaptation has no source connection.#Incorrect. The guide-object pattern remains.
}
